Most recently, organic lead halide perovskite has attracted considerable attention as a prominent light harvester in solar cells because of the excellent properties and the innovative device design.1-4 To utilize this magic material, multiple research groups have demonstrated various device architectures,5-8 among which, mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ solar cells, especially those avoiding using hole transporting materials (HTMs),6,9 have drawn increased interest of several photovoltaic community, due to no requirement of high-cost HTMs and much simpler manufacture process. Since L. Etgar and his co-workers first reported HTMs-free mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ Solar Cells with a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $5.5\%$ , an efficiency of $8.04\%$ was obtained by developing a modified $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ spin-coating method,10 which was further improved to $10.85\%$ with two step deposition method.11 Although these kinds of architectures did not required high-cost HTMs, they still need thermal evaporated noble metal Au as counter electrodes (CEs), which is highly energy consumptive and process complicated. To reduce the cell fabrication cost caused by HTMs and Au CEs at the same time, we have developed hole-conductor free mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ perovskite solar cells with low-cost carbon CEs. By employing one-step drop-coating deposition to infiltrate $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ through screen-printed carbon $/\mathrm{ZrO}_{_{2}}/\mathrm{TiO}_{_{2}}$ films, an initial PCE of $6.64\%$ was achieved.12 This performance has been further improved by optimizing the mesoporous carbon contact13,14 and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ scaffold.15 Recently, a high PCE exceeding $12\%$ was obtained in carbon based devices utilizing sequential solution deposition method,16 and moreover, high stability (over 1000 hours) of this fully printable, hole-conductor free perovskite solar cell was achieved.9 Although the efficiency was still inferior to that employs HTMs and Au metal, the architecture design and outstanding stability indicate a very promising prospect for low-cost photovoltaic technology, encouraging us to further optimize this low-cost fully printable mesoscopic perovskite solar cells.
As a crucial component of mesoscopic perovskite solar cells, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer acts as the selective contact to extract electron as well as the scaffold to sustain the perovskite, which influence the device performance significantly. It was demonstrated that the high chemical capacitance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ under illumination may prevent the quasi-Fermi level of electrons to raise high enough for any given charge density,17 hence decreasing the photovoltage of perovskite solar cells. To suppress this decrease, $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ scaffold with fewer surface and sub-band gap states were successfully utilized.18 On the other side, in dye-sensitized solar cells (DSSCs), surface modification of porous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been well known as an effective method to passivate $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface, blocking the electron recombination from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to the electrolyte (or hole conductor). With this motivation, inorganic $\mathrm{Sb}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ layer was inserted at the interface between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ perovskite to block charge recombination process and the photo catalytic effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}^{19}$ while organic $\mathrm{HOCORNH}_{3}^{+}\mathrm{I}^{-}$ anchor was  introduced between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface and perovskite to tune the perovskite crystal growth and retard charge recombination.20 It’s also noticed that a covalently bonded strong silane monomolecular layer has been used to decrease the back electron transfer on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface in conventional DSSCs,21 and employed to tune the conduction band, surface energy as well as the wetting properties of metal oxides.22,23 In addition, theoretical studies have addressed that the interface electronic structure and passivation are crucial for the perovskite solar cell performance,24,25 which indicate potential profit of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface modification. In this report, we introduce an organic silane self-assembled monolayer (SAM) inserted between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ in hole-conductorfree mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ solar cells based on carbon CEs. The effect of this silane layer on the interface properties is discussed through performing electrochemical and photoluminescence measurements.
Fully printable hole-conductor-free mesoscopic perovskite solar cells were fabricated as the following process. FTO glass was patterned using $Z\mathbf{n}$ and HCl (2M) aqueous solution before used. After cleaning the glass substrate with deionized water, ethanol and acetone using an ultrasonic bath for 15min, successively, a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was prepared by aerosol spray pyrolysis deposition of titanium diisopropoxide bis(acetyl acetonate) solution in ethanol on the glass substrate. Then a $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ porous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, a $1\upmu\mathrm{m}\ Z\mathrm{r}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ spacer layer, and a ${\mathfrak{g}}{\upmu}{\mathrm{m}}$ carbon CE were screen printed on the substrate layer by layer. The $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer was sintered at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30min, and the $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ and carbon layer were sintered at $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 30min. The porous films were immersed in solution of aminopropyltrimethoxysilane $\left(\mathbf{0.o5mM}\right)$ in 2- propanol for several hours to introduce a silane layer on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface, and then the films were rinsed copiously in 2- propanol and dried under $\Nu_{2}$ flow. The deposition of perovskite $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ into the porous films was obtained with a two-step sequential method. $1.5\upmu\mathrm{L}$ 1M solution of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ in DMF was infiltrated into the films by drop-coating, followed by annealing at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30\mathrm{{min}}$ . After that, the films coated with $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ were dipped in 10mg/mL solution of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ in 2- prpanol for about 12min. Once the color of the films turn into dark brown, which indicates the formation of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}.$ , the films was rinsed with 2-propanol.
The schematic structure of a typical hole-conductor free mesoscopic perovskite solar cell based on carbon CE is shown in Figure 1a. All the films including nanoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ insulating layer and mesoscopic carbon CE were screen-printed on to the FTO glass substrate layer by layer. The energy level in Figure 1b allows electron extraction from $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $(-3.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (-4.0 eV) conduction band, and hole collection from $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ valence band $(-5.4\ \mathrm{eV})$ to carbon CE $(-5.0\ \mathrm{eV})$ , while the silane SAM is located between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface and perovskite which will be discussed later. Figure S1 shows a representative scanning electron microscopy (SEM) image observed from the cross section of the monolithic device, where the carbon CE layer can be clearly distinguished from the sublayers, and the $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ layer functions as a spacer to prevent any direct contact between carbon CE and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film.
Actually, the assembly of silane monolayer onto $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface can be realized by forming a covalent bonding through condensation reaction between the hydrolyzed alkoxy of silane and the hydroxyl groups present at the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface. The ATR-FTIR spectrum was measured to specify the formation of silane SAM and the results are shown in Figure 2. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film samples functionalized with silane SAM at different time are characterized by the presence of a broad band in the 3800-3000 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ region, while absorption band at 2929 and $2976~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ can be assigned to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching of - $\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ groups on the alkyl chain. The absorption band in the low frequency region can be assigned to the bending modes of $\left.-\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.$ groups $(1627~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ and $1582~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1})}$ , - $\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ groups (1410 $\mathbf{cm}^{-1},$ ) and Si-O-Si $(\mathbf{1066\cm^{-1}})$ network. The intensity of $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ and $-C\mathrm{H}_{2}$ vibration bands increase with increased treatment time up to 24h. These results indicate that the silane SAM has been successfully introduced onto the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface.
Devices based on different silane treatment time were fabricated with modified two-step sequential method to investigate the effect of coverage extent of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface. The characteristic parameters are summarized in Table S1. Compared with unsilanized cell, silane treated cells show increased open-circuit photovoltaic $(\mathrm{V_{OC}})$ with extended immersing time in silane solution, while the short-circuit photocurrent density $(\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}})$ and PCE reach the maximum values around 1h to 4h. It can be observed that the fill factor $(F F)$ decreases to previous value of 0.69 with 24h’s treatment after it goes over 0.75 at the best performance point.
In order to investigate the significant difference the silane SAM made in device performance, SEM were utilized. Figure $^3$ shows the typical SEM top view images of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film filled with spin-coated perovskite at different treatment time, where high spin rate was applied to avoid any capping layer with excess perovskite. The loading amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ on silane SAM modified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figure 3e-f) is apparently larger and more compact than on unmodified surface (Figure 3cd). Indeed, compared with depositing $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ on unmodified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface, the stronger UV-vis absorption could be found using silane SAM modified $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , as presented in Figure S2. This phenomenon is probably due to the enhanced affinity between these two layers induced by the reported hydrogen bond or electrostatic interaction between the amino groups and the perovskite framework.26 Herein it would benefit the precursor to wet the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface and infiltrate into the porous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ when dropping, and to remain in the pore during following high-speed spin process and dipping process in $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ / propanol solution.
Interestingly, when the treatment time is up to 24h, the hill shaped accumulation of perovskite close to the surface can be observed, as shown in Figure $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , and also some perovskite crystalline tend to grow out of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ pore, forming submicron-sized crystal beyond the surface, as shown in Figure 3g. This behavior may be ascribed to the excessive adsorption of silane on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ that leads to multilayer silane network and decreased pore size. On this condition, the bottleneck of a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ mesopore is easily blocked, resulting in unfavorable infiltration of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ precursor into $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ , which gives a reasonable explanation for the decrease of $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{SC}}$ with 24h treatment to a value even lower than control device. Above results indicate a proper coverage extent of silane on porous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface and an appropriate immersing time can be chosen under the chosen concentration of silane solution.
The representative photocurrent-voltage curves of control device (Device A) and silane SAM treated device (Device B) are presented in Figure 4a with the characteristic parameters in Table S1. The average photovoltaic performance and error bars, with 10 individuals fabricated to guarantee reliability are shown in Figure $S_{3}$ . The devices present high reproducibility with the method reported here. At the optimum treatment time of 1h, an average PCE of $11.7\%$ (max. $12.7\%$ ) was achieved, which is higher than the average control device efficiency of $9.5\%$ (max. $9.9\%$ ). Benefiting from the inserted layer, the average $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ increased from 801mV to around $8_{33\mathrm{mV}}$ , while the average FF increased from $_{\mathbf{0.67}}$ to 0.72. It can be concluded that the performance gets all-round improvement with introduction of silane SAM between porous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with perovskite crystalline. In addition to the enhancement in $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ and FF, the average photocurrent is also increased from $\mathrm{17.7mA/cm}^{2}$ to 19. ${\mathrm{.6mA/cm}}^{2}$ . The incident photo-to-current conversion efficiency (IPCE) response describes the ability turning incident photons to extracted electrons at a given wavelength that is shown in Figure $_{4^{\mathrm{b}}}$ . It can be observed that the IPCE of silane SAM based devices generally reach a higher value than the control devices, which is in reasonable agreement with the measured value of photocurrent-voltage curves.
Capacitance-voltage measurements10,28 were performed to further understand the effect of organic silane inserted layer. From the intercept of the linear regime with the $\mathbf{x}$ -axis of the Mott-Schottky plot shown in Figure $_{4^{\mathrm{c}}}$ , the built-in potential value at the $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ contact can be obtained28. The devices with silane SAM layer present around $37\mathrm{mV}$ higher built-in potential than that without this layer. This difference should be attributed to the decreased work function of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ induced by the surface treatment with terminal electrondonating amine group, which directs the dipole moment away from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface.23,27 Larger built-in potential means enhanced driving force for the separation of photo-generated carriers, and also extended depletion region for efficient suppression of electrons from $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ . Therefore, the introduction of silane SAM between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ layer directly contributes to the output voltage of solar cells. The interfacial recombination resistance was determined by using EIS at an applied voltage (see Figure 4d) with a typical Nyquist plot shown in Figure S4. The recombination resistance extracted from the fitting shows slower back electron transfer in the silane SAM based devices, which justifies the enhanced $\mathrm{v_{oc}}$ . This result is in good agreement with that concluded from the typical dark current curves shown in Figure 4a.
To provide deeper insight into the origin of the effect of organic silane inserted layer, time-resolved photoluminescence (PL) measurements on perovskite-coated $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ were performed to investigate the charge dynamic in systems. Figure $\scriptstyle\mathbf{S}5$ shows the luminescence decays collected at the peak perovskite emission $(765\mathrm{nm})$ . As shown in Figure $\mathsf{S}5\mathsf{a}$ with two-step deposition method here, the PL lifetime is reduced significantly when the perovskite film forms a contact with ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ indicating efficient charge extraction. Comparing the PL decay of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}.$ /perovskite and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{SAM}_{I}$ /perovskite, no significant difference in the quenching rate can be observed (9.14ns and 8.76ns), which may indicate that the electron extraction process is not influenced by introducing silane SAM between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ with perovskite. An interesting and unusual observation arises from the comparison of the PL decay in $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/$ perovskite (16.16ns) and $\mathrm{ZrO_{2}/S A M_{\prime}}$ /perovskite (43.96ns), where the PL decay rate slows down with utilizing silane SAM. The longer chargecarrier lifetime observed with silane SAM indicated a much lower defect concentration, which is beneficial to the hole transporting through $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ layer before reaching carbon back contact and herein contributes to higher fill factor. In addition, to compare with two-step deposition method, the PL of samples obtained with one-step method was also measured (see Figure S4b). No significant difference in the quenching rate can be observed for both $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}/$ perovskite (21.74ns) and $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ /SAM/perovskite (22.04ns), which agrees with the previous results (see Figure S6, Table $S_{2}$ ) that the one-step method based devices with and without silane SAM exhibit almost the same J-V performance, hence indicating a probable influence of silane SAM on the nucleation/growth process of perovskite.
In summary, an organic silane self-assembled monolayer was inserted between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface and perovskite in the hole-conductor-free solar cells based on carbon counter electrode. The silane SAM helps tune the interface electronic structure and passivate the recombination process. As a result, a high efficiency of $12.7\%$ was obtained for $\mathrm{TiO_{2}/S A M/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ based mesoscopic solar cell with sequential deposition method. Surface modification was demonstrated to be an effective method to improve device performance. These results show a promising prospect for the hole-conductor-free mesoscopic perovskite solar cells to approach low-cost photovoltaic industrialization.
